{"reference":"Judges 2","book":"Judges","chapter":2,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"chapter_overview","title":"Overview","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Judges 2:11-23 sets out the recurring pattern that frames the book’s central narratives: Israel turns to other gods, suffers oppression, groans under that distress, receives a judge through whom relief comes, and then returns to corruption after the judge’s death. This passage is a programmatic introduction to what follows, rather than a promise that every later episode will repeat each stage in a mechanically identical way.","verse_refs":["Judges 2:11-23"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_judges_002_17f902ea0a37e5f37207c515","reader_path_judges_002_1e876698ba63f9fa8dc4285c"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"historical_context","title":"The Inhabitants Who Remain","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"The warning in verses 1-5 comes after the varied outcomes described in Judges 1. That earlier account includes possession in some places, failure or inability to remove inhabitants in others, coexistence, and forced labor. The setting should therefore not be reduced either to complete conquest or to no settlement at all; Judges 2 addresses a mixed regional situation in which inhabitants and their altars remained.","verse_refs":["Judges 2:1-5"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_judges_002_4a0cfaad092d6411c0d2356c"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"fact"}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"The Book’s Larger Movement","blocks":[{"id":"block_3","text":"Judges 2 helps orient the reader within the larger book. The book moves from incomplete possession and covenant indictment into recurring deliverance narratives, and then toward accounts of internal idolatry, atrocity, and civil war. The chapter’s account of repeated corruption and temporary rescue thus introduces a larger story whose problems do not simply disappear after individual deliverances.","provenance_refs":["reader_path_judges_002_9f967e17563c257b708dcc5b"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"fact"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}